<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lion Capital of Ashoka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 250 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Sarnath Lion Capital</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lion Capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> is a polished sandstone sculpture of four lions standing back to back, which crowned a pillar erected by the Maurya emperor Ashoka at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>, where the Buddha gave his first sermon. Made around 250 BCE, it was excavated in 1905 and is kept in the Sarnath Museum. Adopted in 1950 as the national emblem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, it also appears on Indian currency.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lion-capital-of-ashoka.jpg" alt="Lion Capital of Ashoka" width="320" height="571" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chrisi1964 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarnath_capital.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
